 # Gradient-based Pre-computed Shading
 
-Also called: ComputeShading, Precomputed Lambertian Shading, View-independent voxel shading
+Also called: ComputeShading, Diffuse Shading Baseline, Precomputed Lambertian Shading, View-independent voxel shading
 
 ## Overview
-Gradient-based Pre-computed Shading is a preprocessing technique that calculates shaded colors for each voxel in a volume dataset using a view-independent Lambertian shading model [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of computing surface normals and lighting interactions per-ray during rendering by storing the result in a separate "shading volume" [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Gradient Estimation from Samples](2180-gradient-estimation-from-samples.md), which provides raw gradient vectors for general use, this method specifically applies a diffuse lighting model to produce final pixel-intensity contributions [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Gradient-Based Surface Shading](5648-gradient-based-surface-shading.md) by operating on the dense voxel grid directly rather than extracting an isosurface mesh [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Gradient-based Pre-computed Shading is a preprocessing technique that computes shaded colors for each voxel using a view-independent Lambertian shading model, allowing for faster rendering by avoiding per-ray normal computation [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from on-the-fly gradient estimation by trading memory for performance, storing the shading result in an additional 3D texture, which implicitly applies a low-pass filter to gradients [S3].
 
